research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-the-mean-value-inequality-is-attained.prompt.md:183:      "quote": "Let $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $m \\ge 1$, let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-interval]]). Let $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $M \\ge 0$ satisfy $$\\lVert f'(t)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M \\qquad \\text{for every } t \\in (a,b).$$ Then $$\\lVert f(b) - f(a)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M\\,(b-a).$$ **No integrability of $f'$ is assumed**, so the theorem applies to every differentiable $f$; that is why it is proved from the scalar mean value theorem rather than from [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]]. [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]] records the comparison between the two routes. **The equality form is not asserted, and for $m \\ge 2$ it is false.** There need be **no** $\\xi \\in (a,b)$ with $f(b)-f(a) = f'(\\xi)(b-a)$; the companion page carries a differentiable witness on $[0,1]$. The $\\xi$ produced in the proof below depends on the fixed vector $u = f(b)-f(a)$ and is a mean value point of the **real** function $t \\mapsto \\langle u, f(t)\\rangle$, not of $f$.",
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-the-mean-value-inequality-is-attained.prompt.md:231:      "source": "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-cex-the-mean-value-inequality-is-attained.prompt.md:327:      "quote": "Let $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $m \\ge 1$, let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-interval]]). Let $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $M \\ge 0$ satisfy $$\\lVert f'(t)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M \\qquad \\text{for every } t \\in (a,b).$$ Then $$\\lVert f(b) - f(a)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M\\,(b-a).$$ **No integrability of $f'$ is assumed**, so the theorem applies to every differentiable $f$; that is why it is proved from the scalar mean value theorem rather than from [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]]. [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]] records the comparison between the two routes. **The equality form is not asserted, and for $m \\ge 2$ it is false.** There need be **no** $\\xi \\in (a,b)$ with $f(b)-f(a) = f'(\\xi)(b-a)$; the companion page carries a differentiable witness on $[0,1]$. The $\\xi$ produced in the proof below depends on the fixed vector $u = f(b)-f(a)$ and is a mean value point of the **real** function $t \\mapsto \\langle u, f(t)\\rangle$, not of $f$.",
