research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:3015:          "source": "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:3519:          "source": "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:3817:          "source": "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:3861:          "quote": "Let $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $m \\ge 1$, let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-interval]]). Let $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $M \\ge 0$ satisfy $$\\lVert f'(t)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M \\qquad \\text{for every } t \\in (a,b).$$ Then $$\\lVert f(b) - f(a)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M\\,(b-a).$$ **No integrability of $f'$ is assumed**, so the theorem applies to every differentiable $f$; that is why it is proved from the scalar mean value theorem rather than from [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]]. [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]] records the comparison between the two routes. **The equality form is not asserted, and for $m \\ge 2$ it is false.** There need be **no** $\\xi \\in (a,b)$ with $f(b)-f(a) = f'(\\xi)(b-a)$; the companion page carries a differentiable witness on $[0,1]$. The $\\xi$ produced in the proof below depends on the fixed vector $u = f(b)-f(a)$ and is a mean value point of the **real** function $t \\mapsto \\langle u, f(t)\\rangle$, not of $f$.",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:5723:          "source": "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:5893:          "quote": "Let $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $m \\ge 1$, let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-interval]]). Let $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $M \\ge 0$ satisfy $$\\lVert f'(t)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M \\qquad \\text{for every } t \\in (a,b).$$ Then $$\\lVert f(b) - f(a)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M\\,(b-a).$$ **No integrability of $f'$ is assumed**, so the theorem applies to every differentiable $f$; that is why it is proved from the scalar mean value theorem rather than from [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]]. [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]] records the comparison between the two routes. **The equality form is not asserted, and for $m \\ge 2$ it is false.** There need be **no** $\\xi \\in (a,b)$ with $f(b)-f(a) = f'(\\xi)(b-a)$; the companion page carries a differentiable witness on $[0,1]$. The $\\xi$ produced in the proof below depends on the fixed vector $u = f(b)-f(a)$ and is a mean value point of the **real** function $t \\mapsto \\langle u, f(t)\\rangle$, not of $f$.",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:6055:          "quote": "Let $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $m \\ge 1$, let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-interval]]). Let $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $M \\ge 0$ satisfy $$\\lVert f'(t)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M \\qquad \\text{for every } t \\in (a,b).$$ Then $$\\lVert f(b) - f(a)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M\\,(b-a).$$ **No integrability of $f'$ is assumed**, so the theorem applies to every differentiable $f$; that is why it is proved from the scalar mean value theorem rather than from [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]]. [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]] records the comparison between the two routes. **The equality form is not asserted, and for $m \\ge 2$ it is false.** There need be **no** $\\xi \\in (a,b)$ with $f(b)-f(a) = f'(\\xi)(b-a)$; the companion page carries a differentiable witness on $[0,1]$. The $\\xi$ produced in the proof below depends on the fixed vector $u = f(b)-f(a)$ and is a mean value point of the **real** function $t \\mapsto \\langle u, f(t)\\rangle$, not of $f$.",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:6103:          "source": "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:6199:          "quote": "Let $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $m \\ge 1$, let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$, and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-vector-valued-functions-limits-and-continuity]], [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], [[def-interval]]). Let $M \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $M \\ge 0$ satisfy $$\\lVert f'(t)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M \\qquad \\text{for every } t \\in (a,b).$$ Then $$\\lVert f(b) - f(a)\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; M\\,(b-a).$$ **No integrability of $f'$ is assumed**, so the theorem applies to every differentiable $f$; that is why it is proved from the scalar mean value theorem rather than from [[thm-norm-inequality-for-the-vector-valued-integral]]. [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]] records the comparison between the two routes. **The equality form is not asserted, and for $m \\ge 2$ it is false.** There need be **no** $\\xi \\in (a,b)$ with $f(b)-f(a) = f'(\\xi)(b-a)$; the companion page carries a differentiable witness on $[0,1]$. The $\\xi$ produced in the proof below depends on the fixed vector $u = f(b)-f(a)$ and is a mean value point of the **real** function $t \\mapsto \\langle u, f(t)\\rangle$, not of $f$.",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:7269:          "source": "def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral",
research/audit/wave12-proof-contracts.json:7408:          "quote": "Let $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $m \\ge 1$, let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a \\le b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}^{m}$ be integrable ([[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]]). Then: 1. if $a < b$, the real-valued function $t \\mapsto \\lVert f(t)\\rVert_2$ is integrable on $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]], [[def-p-norms-on-rn]]); 2. $$\\Bigl\\lVert \\int_a^b f \\Bigr\\rVert_2 \\;\\le\\; \\int_a^b \\lVert f\\rVert_2 .$$ **The hypothesis $a \\le b$ is not decoration.** With the orientation convention of [[def-oriented-integral]] and [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], interchanging the limits changes the sign of the right-hand side but not of the left, so for $b < a$ the correct statement is $\\bigl\\lVert\\int_a^b f\\bigr\\rVert_2 \\le \\bigl|\\int_a^b \\lVert f\\rVert_2\\bigr|$; the displayed inequality as written is false in that case. This is the same trap the scalar inequality of [[cor-integrability-of-absolute-values-products-and-lattice-operations]] carries. **Clause 1 is a genuine obligation and is discharged before the estimate.** That each $f_i$ is integrable does not by itself say that $\\sqrt{\\sum_{i<m} f_i^{2}}$ is; the square root has to be brought in through [[thm-composition-with-a-continuous-function]].",
